on this channel i'm always talking about  combining multiple different forms of  training because i genuinely believe  that that's the best way to get the most  comprehensive fitness and performance  profile i don't think that any one  modality can offer everything you need  to perform your very best one of the  criticisms i sometimes get about this  though is that it's a bit overwhelming  where do you even begin when you have  such a smorgasbord to choose from  kettlebell training bodybuilding  powerlifting calisthenics mobility  long distance running martial arts etc  well a great place to start for a lot of  people would be to take two different  forms of training and to combine them  and as it happens there are many great  pairings that go together like great  cheeses and wines where they perfectly  complement each other make up for what's  missing and compound on those benefits a  while back i made a video called  functional aesthetics where i talked  about how beneficial it could be to  combine functional training you know  traditional functional type movements  with bodybuilding training this way the  functional athletes would get some of  the isolated hypertrophy that's missing  from that kind of training they could  work on the smaller muscle groups that  even functional training might overlook  conversely the bodybuilder would get  some additional aesthetics as well from  things like the obliques that aren't  traditionally hit by bodybuilding  routines at the same time they learn to  combine those different areas of  strength into more compound movements  and complex movement patterns they build  stability balance and all that good  stuff check out that video if you're  interested today i'm talking about  another pairing that's perhaps even more  powerful that's to combine calisthenics  with athletic training  so just to reiterate i'm not saying by  any means this is the very best form of  training this is what i'm going to be  doing from now on this is just one  possible combination of many that  demonstrates how pairing different  workouts different forms of training can  be an excellent approach and these two  really do go together like starsky and  hutch  so what i'm talking about here well  calisthenics of course means body weight  training i'm talking about push-ups air  squats sit-ups etc pull-ups however past  a certain point a true calisthenics  athlete needs to move on to more complex  movements more challenging movements  they're going to be doing things like  hands-down push-ups planche progressions  front levers that kind of thing  meanwhile by athletic training i'm  talking about the kinds of things that a  coach might give to an athlete if they  wanted to improve their performance in  sports so we're talking about things  like hip thrusts bounds sprinting drills  we're talking about depth jumps and box  jumps perhaps some knees over toes stuff  like a deep atg split squat we're  talking various cable exercises  rotational movements that kind of thing  banded lateral walks bounds  anterior reaches that kind of stuff and  there's loads of reasons why these  things go together like sonic and tails  the one thing that both these types of  training have in common is they don't  pack on too much weight too much mass an  athlete doesn't want to be extremely  bulky for the majority of activities  because otherwise it's going to slow  them down it's going to get in the way  and whilst you can be powerful and  explosive and have a lot of muscle on  the whole this kind of training is going  to lead to slower performance if nothing  else it's going to land you in a higher  weight class which can be detrimental  for many different sports of course your  calisthenics athletes don't want to pack  on too much weight because it's much  harder to do a full planche or a  one-armed pull-up if you weigh more so  in both scenarios we want a high  strength to weight ratio this is  something they have in common however  one thing that's missing often from  calisthenics training that athletics can  offer is leg training  now before you come at me and tell me  that there's lots of great leg exercises  in calisthenics of course you have  things like pistol squats [ __ ] squats  air squats squat jumps lunges etc the  truth is that in order to really build  mass on your legs it's often a lot  easier to add extra weight and you can  build some decent legs but this isn't  something that calisthenics is known for  let's put it that way and at the same  time legs in particular if you want to  do things like planche getting them too  big and bulky really can be detrimental  to your performance so you do tend to  see that calisthenics athletes sometimes  have slightly smaller legs in comparison  to their upper body however this is  where athletic training can fit like a  glove because this is going to come in  and give you a way to train your legs  that isn't focused on building massive  bulk but gives you even more balance  even more stability explosiveness  running speed you're going to add power  and strength to the legs but not in the  way it's going to lead to massive  thunder thighs or going to make you  really heavy or going to negatively  impact on your mobility  at the same time this kind of training  often uses body weight or just a little  bit of extra weight often about 30  percent of body weight which is optimal  for increasing explosiveness across many  different exercises and studies  this is a perfect way to train legs as a  calisthenics athlete that's going to  give you this whole extra dimension to  your performance because now you're also  running super fast and jumping super  high and darting and cutting and  changing direction on top of being able  to do things like one arm pull-ups and  plants you're just going to be this  athletic performance machine and adding  athletic training to your calisthenics  routine is also a great way to get some  cardio in or some high-intensity  interval training because once again you  can just simply combine steady-state  cardio with calisthenics or you can just  perform high-high repetition push-ups in  a fashion that makes it more like a form  of cardio but i think doing these  high-intensity interval training you  know sprints and drags and med ball  slams in the fashion that you would do  for athletic training is a really good  option too  and in a similar manner something else  that's often missing from calisthenics  training is rotational strength i've  talked on this channel before about how  important rotational strength and power  can be and this isn't just calisthenics  it's missing from so much training  whether it's power lifting whether it's  bodybuilding we so rarely twist our  bodies in training even though it's  something we do all the time in real  life and in sports whether you're  wrestling someone whether you're pushing  or pulling pretty much anything and even  just walking irritate your body when  you're throwing training the serape  effect building powerful hips and a  rotational core this is all absolutely  crucial i think to over performance and  it's the perfect example of why picking  just one form of training often isn't  enough i like to do kick throughs but  there's not a ton of option you can do  with calisthenics training however if  you look at the average athletics  training program it often does include  lots of stuff for rotation and not just  anti-rotation like you might get from  calisthenics from things like one-armed  push-ups etc but actually rotating and  twisting the body against resistance  whether it's something like a cable wood  chop or even something like a rotational  med ball slam once again you're plugging  a gap that's missing from calisthenics  with something really cool from  athletics  so now you might be thinking you can get  lots of benefits as a calisthenics  athlete by adding athletic style  training but what benefit does the  athletic trainee get from adding  calisthenics  well first of all calisthenics is  fantastic for building an extremely  strong core this is something that  already exists in sports training and  athletics however if you use  calisthenics you're going to build it to  a whole extra level as stuart mcgill  says it's really important to have a  powerful core because this allows you to  express force and power through your  limbs  i always use the analogy of hitting  someone with a floppy stick with a nail  on the end versus hitting someone with a  hard plank of wood with a nail on the  end you're going to transform more power  via the hard plank of wood because it's  not leaking energy  and then calisthenics are doing all  these moves that require an incredibly  powerful and stable core this is one of  the reasons why gymnastics is often  thought of as one of the activities that  has the most transfer to all the other  forms of sport and athletics so this  would just be a way for an athlete or  someone who's interested in sports to  take their performance potentially to  the next level at the same time  athletics training on its own doesn't  necessarily offer a lot of hypertrophy  and muscle building in a more isolated  and traditional sense you're not just  bulking up specific muscle groups  because there's not enough isolation in  there  whilst you might do squats and deadlifts  the aim here is not hypertrophy at the  same time you're not going to be doing  lots and lots of curls most likely  however calisthenics is excellent for  building muscle whilst you might not  pack on the bulk that you might get from  bodybuilding or powerlifting  calisthenics can build some of the very  best physiques going not only because of  that really powerful core but because of  the intense work you do on the shoulders  and the lats and because of the fact  that you're working with your own body  weight which leads to a really ripped  and lean physique  and the other big way in which athletic  training can benefit from adding some  calisthenics is simply by creating a  much more well-rounded athlete because  athletic training often means training  for a particular sport or event and  sometimes this can mean not getting the  full package becoming too specialist in  any one area can be a recipe for  imbalances and injuries and it can lead  to problems down the road even if you're  using a more broad athletic style  training without necessarily having a  particular sport in mind you might not  be doing things like training the biceps  in a big way or the triceps or the  shoulders or even the lats because you  might rarely be hanging so by adding  calisthenics you get this far more  well-rounded physique that plugs all of  those gaps once again and at the same  time isn't going to add lots of bulk and  isn't going to slow you down because  you're using your own body weight both  these styles of training are about  becoming an absolute master of your body  weight it's just that calisthenics is  about defying gravity and controlling  movement in space whereas athletic  training is about being explosive fast  and nimble and by combining these two  things you can create a truly powerful  and well-rounded athlete and then once  you've done that you can start looking  at adding a little bit of kettlebell  training bodybuilding etc as well to  become even more rounded still just to  be absolutely clear it's not like you  can't get almost all of these benefits  from doing a purely calisthenics-based  workout or from doing a purely  athletics-based workout there are plenty  of exercises for the legs if you're  doing calisthenics like i've said it's  more just that this gives you a bigger  pool to select from and where there  might be a slightly more limited  selection in calisthenics you get a  wider selection in athletics and in many  cases the other way around and that's  why these two  mesh together so nicely  if you're looking for a program that  combines multiple different styles of  training that includes things from  athletic training from calisthenics then  of course you can try my ebook and  training program super functional  training 2.0 the protein performance  system and that also comes with over two  hours of instructional training videos  that also incorporates things like  kettlebell training mobility work brain  training all in a systematic fashion  that's adaptable to any ability level  and nearly any kind of equipment so i'll  put the link to that in the description  down below if you're interested either  way thank you so much for watching this  one guys i massively appreciate it let  me know in the comments down below do  you think that this kind of athletic  training plus calisthenics is a good mix  another big one for me is powerlifting  with kettlebell training and i might  talk about that in a future video if  this is something that people find  interesting subscribe if you want to see  more like this hit the bell button for  notifications and like and share around  if you want to that helps me out  massively and thank you so much for  watching this one guys  bye for now

